    "text": "The refactoring has substantial internal complexity. Let me work through the design decisions before writing code.\n\n**Design notes**\n\n1. **Process supervisor (`benchkit/process.py`)** \u2014 Both tools spawn children (engine + `nvidia-smi`, or a verify binary) and both have the same failure mode: an exception mid-run can orphan children. The original code partially handles this with a `finally` block, but the watchdog timer races with normal completion, `nvidia-smi` is terminated but never waited, and the verify tool has no cleanup at all. I'll build one supervisor that:\n   - owns a `subprocess.Popen` with line-buffered stdout, a log sink, and a watchdog timer,\n   - guarantees termination in `close()`/context exit (kill \u2192 wait, with a grace timeout),\n   - exposes `readline()` that raises `EngineError` on EOF or `ERR` lines (preserving the original semantics: EOF message includes the poll code, `ERR` lines propagate verbatim),\n   - exposes `send()` for stdin writes and `wait()` for exit-code checks,\n   - supports multiple concurrent children (engine + GPU sampler) via a small registry so `close()` tears down all of them. I'll make the",
